with a nine and five record the Atlanta  Falcons had in tomorrow's game with the  New Orleans Saints in the thick of the  NFL's postseason race ball team owner  Arthur Blank has built the Falcons into  a championship contender he has also  been the driving force behind the  construction of a world-class Stadium  for the team to play in but blank isn't  finished with his rebuilding plan  he hopes the stadium will be the  catalyst for change for an entire city  mercedes-benz stadium the 1.5 billion  dollar state-of-the-art home to the  NFL's Falcons and Major League Soccer's  Atlanta United from its elaborately  designed eight panel retractable roof to  its all inspiring 11 hundred foot halo  scoreboard it may look like a sporting  cathedral but according to the Falcons  president and CEO rich McKay it was  built to be more like a community center  Arthur's theory going in was let's not  look at Stadium locations let's not go  look at where we might put a stadium for  the best economics let's realize we're  gonna put the stadium downtown and we're  gonna try to put it right in the heart  of downtown the stadium is bordered by a  thriving commercial district to the  north and a dilapidated neighborhood to  the west over the years everybody has  thought or said build a stadium here it  will bring money to a city is that true  doesn't work not true what we were  saying to people was this stadium can be  a catalyst for change we can bring  everybody to the table we can make an  impact on the Westside but it won't just  be because we build the building once  home to civil rights legends like Martin  Luther King jr. and Julian Bond  Atlanta's historic West Side has become  one of the poorest neighborhoods in the  United States one that Falcons owner  Arthur Blank a co-founder of the Home  Depot wanted to help rebuild  when we began designed the stadium I  said you know we're gonna be committed  to the communities nearby particularly  on the west side which you know probably  thirty years ago had a population of  55,000 people it's down to about 10  percent of that now all the things you  look for in a healthy neighborhood they  had and then for a variety of reasons  that's gone downhill four years ago the  Arthur M Blanc Family Foundation started  dedicating substantial money and  resources to the West  Side neighborhood 37 million dollars has  already been committed that has some  longtime residents worrying about  gentrification others think real change  still will never come how much  skepticism did you get from a community  that obviously had been promised a lot  before a lot how do you deal with that I  think in my own case I had a fair amount  of credibility in Atlanta because of my  history in history of a foundation and I  think our early behavior they could see  was different is that we weren't there  to write write checks we were there to  understand the issues and then to  respond to the issues and respond to  them in a way that's really meaningful  this neighborhood is literally  overlooking the stadium behind us mm-hmm  it has some of the best it really has  the best view of the stadium in the city  Frank Fernandez was part of that  response hired to serve as a community  liaison for the Foundation's Westside  development project you can see  buildings that are been and you have  overgrown you have sidewalks that aren't  maintained you got busted out lights  Fernandez took us through the West Side  neighborhood if you were a person who  lives in poverty or a child born into  poverty in Atlanta you have only a 4%  chance moving out of poverty to the  higher income brackets and so our  commitment is geography should not  constitute destiny right and so how do  we help break that cycle of poverty and  how do we use this the stadium as a  catalyst for change the Foundation's  answer investing in the infrastructure  and people Atlanta's YMCA has relocated  its headquarters to the west side the  foundation has built homes for police  officers so they can live in the  neighborhood not just patrol it and  sponsors in the stadium were strongly  encouraged to invest in the community as  well like chick-fil-a and they're gonna  employ 80 people from the community at  this store all of our corporate partners  all 13 of them have committed to working  with us in the West Side have committed  nearly 15 million dollars one of the  things Frank said to us it it's not  going in there and saying we're fixing  this this place will do this it's doing  it together  they have to buy into it the end of the  day the answer can't be what we're  imposing on them we want them to say  these to make sense for us  and these two don't make sense for us so  we'll do the two that makes sense for  them  what's your measurement here seven seven  like Westside works a neighborhood  program started by the foundation  providing job training in fields like  Technology nursing construction and  culinary arts according to the group  more than 500 people have already been  placed in jobs that have brought in ten  and a half million dollars in income  people like Westside resident Tony  Thomas who one day hopes to have her own  food truck do you feel like it's changed  some attitudes because you're seeing  actual changes yeah because now people  they have hope now at first it wasn't  having hope so now you have a lot of  people that have a hope Thomas works at  the West nest in Mercedes Benz Stadium a  concession stand operated by fellow  graduates West nest and concessions  throughout the arena offer prices you  don't see in most professional sporting  facilities like five dollar beers along  with two dollar hot dogs and refillable  sodas where did that idea come from to  just say let's give people food that's  affordable in a stadium number one I was  always annoyed and irritated as a father  having to gone to stadiums and events  and paying you know ridiculous prices  for everything in the building and so  I'd hear fans just people around me just  complaining about it all the time so I  you know said time management think we  need to price it every day the same way  they would pay any place else they went  in the city of Atlanta you're a  businessman this sounds like this could  also help your bottom line and so we you  know I mean I think over time I think  we'll see that our volumes interrupts  dramatically but they weren't you know  Dana one of the reasons that we wanted  to do this was not just for the  mercedes-benz stadium is that if we can  rationalize and show people that it's  not only the right things for the fans  but it's the right thing for the  business as well we're hoping that we'll  set an example for other people not only  in Atlanta oh really but throughout the  United States blank says doing the right  thing is also at the heart of the West  Side Development Project as for  measuring those results so when is the  west side of success well it's it's I  think it's directionally a success now  you'll see the changes come over time  I mean my guests over the next you know  three four or five years you continue to  see very significant progress in a  variety of areas as you move towards  long long term solutions so I'm very  optimistic
 

